# Remediation Orchestration Memory

Apply this rule whenever planning, delegating, implementing, reviewing, or reporting work from
`docs/reports/thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review-main.md`.

## Freeze a work package before delegation

- Map it to exact report sections and semantic invariants.
- Record current facts, required deliverables, exact file inventory, non-goals, validation commands,
  expected test counts, and static-search acceptance conditions.
- Build the inventory before assigning an agent; never use "all remaining fixtures" without listing
  or mechanically identifying them.
- Prefer one closable semantic invariant over a task scoped to one symptom or one line of code.

## Give agents a hard Definition of Done

- State allowed production areas, forbidden shortcuts, platform requirements, and documentation work.
- Require every discovered occurrence to be classified as a production path, assembler-driven test,
  narrow physical harness, or semantic-boundary negative test.
- A final report must list modified files, disposition of the frozen inventory, static-search results,
  exact commands, expected/actual test counts, and unverified platform gaps.
- "Partially complete" is valid. An agent must not claim completion while an inventory item or required
  gate remains open.
- Metal device validation must run outside the sandbox when sandbox execution cannot see `MTLDevice`;
  lack of a Metal device inside the sandbox is not a blocker.

## Review without changing scope

- Review first against the frozen package Definition of Done.
- Separate findings into `current-package blockers` and `later remediation items`.
- Do not make a real but out-of-scope architectural debt block a narrower package.
- Do not lower semantic, ownership, authority, or Stage 0--7 requirements merely to obtain green tests.
- Consolidate all in-scope findings into one return-to-agent pass; avoid serial one-finding microtasks.

## Control progress and context

- Maintain a remediation ledger: report item, owner, status, evidence, remaining gap, and next dependency.
- Use fresh agents for newly frozen packages instead of repeatedly extending an agent with accumulated,
  conflicting scope. Reuse an agent only for one bounded correction pass.
- Close each package at four levels: implemented, locally verified, platform-verified, documented.
- Do not start the next dependent package until the current package's required levels are closed.
- Optimize for closing report invariants, not for maximizing test count or minimizing diff size.

